CoCo SDC regression tests
=========================

These run 6809 test programs under XRoar's CoCo SDC emulation and compare
what they report with the expected results.

    ./run-tests.sh [-k] [-u] XROAR SDCDOS-ROM [TEST...]

XROAR is an XRoar binary built with --enable-experimental, SDCDOS-ROM the
CoCo 3 build of SDC-DOS (bank 0 of the cartridge's flash, without the
5-byte header it has on the SDC setup disk).  Tests run on an emulated
CoCo 3, so XRoar needs its CoCo 3 ROMs.  The suite needs lwasm, decb
(ToolShed) and python3.  With no TEST, every test is run.

-k keeps each test's working directory (a failing test's is always kept).
-u writes each test's output to its expect.txt instead of comparing: check
the differences before committing them.

Tests
-----

Each directory is a test:

    t.bas       BASIC program, run from T.DSK in drive 0
    *.asm       assembled and copied to T.DSK as NAME.BIN
    setup.sh    optional: fills the SD card directory, sd
    run.sh      optional: runs XRoar and prints the results
    expect.txt  the expected results

The SD card starts holding T.DSK, mounted in drive 0 by STARTUP.CFG.
setup.sh and run.sh are sourced by run-tests.sh in the test's working
directory, with these helpers:

    mkdsk IMG [FILE...]  make a Disk BASIC image holding FILEs
    mksdf [OPT...] IMG   make an SDF image (see mksdf.py)
    xr [ARG...]          run XRoar on sd, for $TIMEOUT emulated seconds
    out_txt [IMG]        print OUT.TXT from IMG (default sd/T.DSK)

setup.sh can set TYPE (what's typed into BASIC, default RUN"T") and
TIMEOUT (default 60).  Without run.sh, a test runs XRoar typing TYPE and
prints OUT.TXT.  Files on the SD card are given a fixed date, as the SDC
reports dates in directory listings.

    crc       reading sectors with bad ID or data CRCs, or missing
    diskset   '+' and '#' with a disk set
    flash     KILL MEM / WRITE MEM, and the flash file
    hidden    names containing '~' are hidden
    names     M:, N: and D: name matching, wildcards, long names
    newsdf    creating SDF images with N:
    sdctime   time taken by each command (the timing model)
    settime   time taken by disk set commands
    snapshot  snapshots taken mid-command restore and finish the same
    version   the firmware version
    wp        write protection from SDF headers and read-only files

sdctime and settime were written to measure a real SDC, and their source
says how.  On hardware they report somewhat different times: the SD card's
own times vary from card to card.
